<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Korea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Democratic People's Republic of Korea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the Democratic People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>, is the state in the north of the Korean peninsula proclaimed in 1948. It grew out of the Soviet occupation zone north of the 38th parallel, where the Soviet authorities brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> to power. Ruled since by the Kim family and the Workers' Party, it invaded the South in 1950, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-korea.jpg" alt="North Korea" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EuropeanV · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Korea_(20-33).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> came under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which had won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Japan compelled Korea to become its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1905 and formally annexed it in 1910. A period of forced assimilation followed, in which Korean language, culture and history were suppressed. It led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a> protests in 1919 and to resistance groups in exile, primarily in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>In the 1930s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> developed mines, hydro-electric dams and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mills in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, so that 65% of Korea's heavy industry but only 37% of its agriculture lay in the north. A Korean guerrilla movement emerged in the mountainous interior and in Manchuria, and one of its most prominent leaders was the Communist Kim Il Sung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Japanese surrender in 1945 the peninsula was divided into two occupation zones along the 38th parallel, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> holding the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the south. Negotiations on reunification failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> became chairman of the Provisional People's Committee of North Korea, established in February 1946. The South declared its statehood in May 1948, and the Democratic People's Republic of Korea was established in the North on 9 September 1948, with Kim Il Sung as premier.</p><p>Shtykov and Kim lobbied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> to support a quick war against the South, and it began in 1950 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>. The Korean Armistice Agreement of 1953 brought a ceasefire and established the Korean Demilitarized Zone. Kim Il Sung consolidated power and promoted his philosophy of Juche as the state ideology. The loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> trading partners and natural disasters in the 1990s brought a famine from 1995 to 1998, which an estimated 240,000 to 420,000 North Koreans did not survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> occupies the northern part of the Korean Peninsula, covering 120,540 square kilometres. About 80 percent of it is mountains and uplands, and the highest point is Paektu Mountain at 2,744 metres, a sacred place in Korean culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> is the capital and largest city. The Workers' Party of Korea is the sole ruling party and the state owns almost all means of production. The Historic Monuments and Sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complex-of-koguryo-tombs">Complex of Koguryo Tombs</a> are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North Korea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goguryeo grew out of the Yemaek peoples of the middle Yalu and Hun river valleys, a rugged region of mountains and narrow farmland on the borders of modern China and <b>North Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan carried out the first modern archaeological excavations in Korea, and the Governor-General's law of 1933 to preserve historical artifacts is the origin of the National Treasures of both South and <b>North Korea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Megalithic burials, Mumun pottery and large settlements found in the Liao River Basin and <b>North Korea</b> around 1800–1500 BCE probably point to its origins.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Communist parties govern China, Vietnam, Laos and Cuba as one-party states, and <b>North Korea</b> has been led by three generations of the Kim family since 1948; Myanmar’s armed forces seized power in a coup in February 2021, and soldiers have taken power in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese Communist Party proclaimed the People’s Republic of China on 1 October 1949, and communist parties came to rule <b>North Korea</b>, Vietnam, Cuba and Laos.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The name alone guarantees little: in <b>North Korea</b>, and in Syria under the Assad family, power passed from father to son although neither state was formally a monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
